**Proof.**

Let $S = \sum_{k=1}^{n} a_k$ and $Q = \sum_{k=1}^{n} a_k^{2}$. The hypothesis gives
$$A + Q < \frac{S^{2}}{n-1}, \qquad\text{i.e.,}\qquad A < \frac{S^{2}-(n-1)Q}{n-1}.$$
It suffices to show that for every pair $1\le i<j\le n$,
$$\frac{S^{2}-(n-1)Q}{n-1}\;\le\; 2\,a_i a_j, \tag{$\ast$}$$
since then $A < 2a_i a_j$ follows immediately.

---

**Case $n=2$.** Here $S^{2}-Q = (a_1+a_2)^{2}-(a_1^{2}+a_2^{2}) = 2a_1a_2 = 2(n-1)a_1a_2$, so $(\ast)$ holds with equality, and the strict hypothesis yields $A<2a_1a_2$. ✓

---

**Case $n\ge 3$.** Fix a pair $i<j$ and set
$$u = a_i+a_j,\qquad T = \sum_{k\ne i,j} a_k,\qquad R = \sum_{k\ne i,j} a_k^{2},$$
so that $S = u+T$ and $Q = a_i^{2}+a_j^{2}+R$. Since $2a_ia_j = u^{2}-a_i^{2}-a_j^{2}$, the desired inequality $(\ast)$, namely
$$S^{2}-(n-1)Q \le 2(n-1)\,a_ia_j,$$
is equivalent (after expanding $S^{2}=(u+T)^{2}$ and substituting $2(n-1)a_ia_j = (n-1)u^{2}-(n-1)(a_i^{2}+a_j^{2})$ and canceling the common term $-(n-1)(a_i^{2}+a_j^{2})$) to
$$2uT + T^{2} - (n-1)R \;\le\; (n-2)\,u^{2},$$
or equivalently
$$2uT - (n-2)\,u^{2} \;\le\; (n-1)R - T^{2}. \tag{$\ast\ast$}$$

We establish $(\ast\ast)$ via two standard inequalities.

*Cauchy–Schwarz* applied to the $n-2$ terms $\{a_k : k\ne i,j\}$ gives
$$T^{2} = \Bigl(\sum_{k\ne i,j} a_k\Bigr)^{2} \le (n-2)\sum_{k\ne i,j} a_k^{2} = (n-2)R,$$
hence
$$(n-1)R - T^{2} \;\ge\; (n-1)R - (n-2)R \;=\; R. \tag{1}$$

*AM–GM* (the identity $(n-2)u^{2} + \tfrac{T^{2}}{n-2} - 2uT = \bigl(\sqrt{n-2}\,u - \tfrac{T}{\sqrt{n-2}}\bigr)^{2}\ge 0$) gives
$$2uT \;\le\; (n-2)\,u^{2} + \frac{T^{2}}{n-2},$$
so
$$2uT - (n-2)\,u^{2} \;\le\; \frac{T^{2}}{n-2} \;\le\; \frac{(n-2)R}{n-2} \;=\; R, \tag{2}$$
where the last step uses $T^{2}\le (n-2)R$ again.

Combining (1) and (2):
$$2uT - (n-2)\,u^{2} \;\le\; R \;\le\; (n-1)R - T^{2},$$
which is precisely $(\ast\ast)$. Therefore $(\ast)$ holds for every pair $i<j$.

---

Since $A < \dfrac{S^{2}-(n-1)Q}{n-1} \le 2\,a_i a_j$ for all $1\le i<j\le n$, the proof is complete. $\blacksquare$

